      "text": "The given matrices are described by \"add 2 and then repeat\", which means that each matrix is equal to the identity matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\). The problem can be simplified by observing the algebraic identity \\((1+x)(1+y) = 1 + (x+y) + xy\\).\n\nTo solve the problem, we start with the first matrix:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\nThe next matrix is the same:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nApplying the identity to these two matrices, we get:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 1 & 1 + 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nNext, we consider the product of the third and fourth matrices in the sequence:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 + 4 & 2 + 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe continue this process until the last matrix:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 + 99 & 6 + 99 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 105 & 105 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 105 & 105 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}}.\n\\]",
